<file path="docs/ARCHITECTURE.md">
# Architecture

The API registers a project/session (video plus raw notes) and streams the source
through a storage port. A persistent job is queued; one in-process worker invokes
ffprobe outside the request and persists results or errors.

The database row is the durable job source of truth; the queue only wakes the
worker. Pending jobs are re-enqueued on startup. UUIDs and storage URIs prevent
consumers from depending on paths. Full notes and parsed units are immutable,
reinforced by database triggers. SHA-256 supports future deduplication.

Known limits: local storage and one API replica; no quotas, auth, remote storage,
or distributed leases. pgvector waits until embeddings actually exist.

## Job execution and recovery

The runner resolves a typed processor factory from ProcessingJob.job_type.
initial_index remains responsible for ffprobe and session readiness;
transcription is a separate durable job. This preserves independent failure,
retry and observability boundaries for long stages.

The runner updates heartbeat_at from a separate database session while a
processor runs. At startup it re-enqueues pending jobs and resets stale
processing jobs to pending. Staleness is configurable. attempt_count increments
when execution is claimed, max_attempts bounds recovery and explicit retry, and
jobs beyond the limit become failed. PostgreSQL is authoritative; the asyncio
queue only wakes the single worker.

There is deliberately no distributed claim or lease. Running multiple replicas
could execute the same job concurrently, so deployment is limited to one API
replica/worker until distributed coordination is implemented.

## Transcription pipeline

POST /sessions/{id}/transcriptions ensures a Transcription and queues a
transcription job, returning 202. The provider port isolates the processor from
FasterWhisperProvider and lets tests use deterministic fakes. The real provider
loads models only on demand, reuses the active model, and runs blocking model
and iterator work in threads.

The source media URI is resolved and passed directly to faster-whisper. PyAV
performs decoding, avoiding a redundant derived audio file at this stage.
Segments are persisted in configurable batches with numeric second offsets.
Word timestamps, when enabled, are stored as JSON on their segment.

A configuration fingerprint prevents duplicate equivalent transcriptions while
allowing another model or materially different settings. Partial batches may
exist while status is processing or failed. Every retry first deletes all
segments belonging to that same Transcription, then restarts sequence numbering;
only a fully consumed provider stream marks it completed.

MediaSession.status describes ingestion and basic metadata readiness. Derived
transcription state lives on Transcription and its ProcessingJob, so ASR failure
does not make an otherwise usable session failed.
</file>

<file path="docs/DOMAIN_MODEL.md">
# Domain model

- MediaProject groups sessions.
- MediaAsset owns logical URI, filename, checksum, size and probe metadata.
- MediaSession binds a project, primary media and the exact note document.
- Note is an ordered immutable unit parsed from that document.
- ProcessingJob persists status, stage, progress and error.

Consumers use media_id plus future temporal coordinates and never interpret
storage_uri. A repeated project-scoped Idempotency-Key returns the existing
session and job.

## Transcription

- Transcription represents one provider/configuration execution for a
  MediaAsset. It records project, session and media identity; provider and
  model; requested/detected language; lifecycle timestamps; duration; provider
  metadata; configuration and its fingerprint.
- TranscriptSegment belongs to one Transcription and records a unique sequence,
  start_seconds, end_seconds, text, selected confidence metrics and optional
  word timestamps as JSON.
- ProcessingJob optionally points to a Transcription and additionally records
  heartbeat_at, attempt_count and max_attempts.

The unique (media_id, configuration_fingerprint) constraint is the durable
idempotency boundary. The fingerprint currently covers provider, model,
requested language, beam size, VAD, word timestamps and compute type. The same
media may therefore retain multiple transcriptions when those semantics differ.

Segment offsets are numeric seconds. Future footage retrieval can use
media_id + start_seconds + end_seconds without learning storage layout.

Session status is intentionally coarse: pending covers ingestion and basic
indexing, ready means source and technical metadata are available, and
failed means that initial path failed. Transcription uses its own
pending/processing/completed/failed status and cannot demote a ready session.
</file>

<file path="docs/ROADMAP.md">
# Roadmap

## Implemented

Stage 1 foundation, Stage 2 local ingestion/ffprobe, and deterministic Stage 3
note parsing.

Timestamped local ASR is implemented as an independent durable processing stage:

- typed job dispatch, heartbeats, stale recovery and bounded attempts;
- provider-neutral transcription port with lazy local faster-whisper;
- persisted Transcription and paginated TranscriptSegment records;
- optional word timestamps and selected ASR metrics;
- configuration-based idempotency and retry-safe incremental batches;
- explicit asynchronous API trigger and query endpoints.

## Planned

1. Scene detection, representative frames and managed derived assets.
2. Searchable temporal segments, then embeddings and pgvector.
3. Candidate retrieval for notes and footage.
4. Provider-neutral multimodal alignment where unmatched is valid.
5. Consolidated Semantic Timeline export and project/session Media Search.

Editorial work stays in Ideas Hub; voice, planning, composition and rendering
stay in production services.

There is still no scene detection, representative-frame extraction, embeddings,
pgvector, semantic search, candidate retrieval, Gemini alignment or consolidated
Semantic Timeline.
</file>

